WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:00.440
All right.

00:00.440 --> 00:04.800
So for this project as well, we have a roadmap where we're going to progress through different types

00:04.800 --> 00:06.160
of steps and analyses.

00:06.160 --> 00:11.760
So we're going to do many stuff like change over time, cumulative analysis performance data segmentations

00:11.760 --> 00:14.480
and other end reporting and all using SQL.

00:14.480 --> 00:16.480
So let's start with the first step in the roadmap.

00:16.760 --> 00:19.440
We're going to analyze the change over time.

00:19.440 --> 00:20.120
So let's go.

00:24.040 --> 00:25.800
Okay so now what is change over time.

00:25.800 --> 00:30.280
It is a technique in order to analyze how a measure evolves over the time.

00:30.280 --> 00:35.760
And this is very important in order to track the trends and as well to identify seasonality of your

00:35.760 --> 00:36.160
data.

00:36.160 --> 00:38.000
And the formula for that is very simple.

00:38.000 --> 00:42.920
We're going to go and aggregate a measure, but this time based on a date dimension.

00:42.920 --> 00:47.840
For example, the total sales by a year, the average cost by the month.

00:47.840 --> 00:52.920
So if you combine an aggregated measure together with a date, column or dimension, then all what you

00:52.960 --> 00:56.240
are doing is you are analyzing the change over time.

00:56.240 --> 01:00.160
So for example, we're going to go and break our measure this time for example, by the years.

01:00.160 --> 01:05.500
And with that we can track immediately how our business is doing over the time over the years.

01:05.500 --> 01:08.780
So for example, we can see here the best year was 2024.

01:08.820 --> 01:12.380
And then we have really hard decline in our business in 2025.

01:12.420 --> 01:15.380
And then slightly it's going up in 2026.

01:15.380 --> 01:18.740
So with that we can quickly analyze the trends of our business.

01:18.740 --> 01:22.940
So now let's go and check the trends and the changes over time in our business okay.

01:22.980 --> 01:26.660
So now let's analyze the trends and changes over time in our data.

01:26.660 --> 01:32.740
And in order to do this kind of analysis usually we target the fact table because there usually we have

01:32.740 --> 01:34.740
our measures and as well dates.

01:34.740 --> 01:37.540
So we have the order date shipping date and due date.

01:37.580 --> 01:42.060
Now what we can do we can go and analyze there the sales performance over time.

01:42.060 --> 01:45.780
So as we learned all what we need is a metric and a date.

01:45.820 --> 01:49.220
Let's go for example and select the order date.

01:49.700 --> 01:54.620
And as well one of those measures sales amount from our fact table.

01:55.940 --> 01:57.780
So let's go and query it.

01:57.820 --> 02:02.660
And we can go and order the data by the order date ascending.

02:02.860 --> 02:04.260
So let's go and execute.

02:04.260 --> 02:06.530
And as you can see we have nulls in our data.

02:06.530 --> 02:09.250
What we can do, we can go and filter those data out.

02:09.250 --> 02:10.170
We don't need it.

02:10.450 --> 02:13.530
So we're going to say where order date is not null.

02:13.570 --> 02:15.210
So let's go and execute it again.

02:15.250 --> 02:15.730
All right.

02:15.730 --> 02:17.730
So that we don't have those orders.

02:17.770 --> 02:21.130
Now as you can see we have sales over time right.

02:21.130 --> 02:23.250
We have a date and we have a measure.

02:23.250 --> 02:24.890
So this looks really good.

02:24.930 --> 02:28.730
But now what we're going to do we're going to go and aggregate the data by the sales amount.

02:28.730 --> 02:30.690
So let's go and say sum.

02:31.410 --> 02:33.570
And we're going to call it total sales.

02:33.890 --> 02:37.170
And then we group up the data by the order dates.

02:37.770 --> 02:39.330
So let's go and execute it.

02:39.330 --> 02:43.330
And with that as you can see for each day we have the total sales.

02:43.330 --> 02:46.210
So now the granularity of our data is the day.

02:46.210 --> 02:49.970
And we can say of course now we are analyzing the sales over time.

02:49.970 --> 02:53.090
But usually we don't aggregate the data on the day level.

02:53.090 --> 02:55.250
We want to have higher aggregations.

02:55.250 --> 02:57.290
For example, let's go to the years.

02:57.330 --> 03:03.650
And now, in order to change the dimension date here from a day to a year, we have to use date functions.

03:03.650 --> 03:07.890
And there are a lot of date functions in order to extract that date part.

03:07.930 --> 03:13.470
And now in order just to get the year, we have a quick function called year and it's going to convert

03:13.470 --> 03:14.950
our date to year.

03:14.950 --> 03:17.390
So let's call it order year.

03:17.390 --> 03:20.910
And of course we have to go and group up the data by the year.

03:20.910 --> 03:22.790
And that will sort it by the year.

03:22.790 --> 03:24.430
So let's go and execute.

03:24.470 --> 03:28.110
Now we are at the year level and we have only five years.

03:28.110 --> 03:31.950
So that means we have changed the aggregation from the day to year.

03:31.990 --> 03:37.070
And now it is very easy to analyze the performance of our business over the years.

03:37.070 --> 03:39.190
So the first year was the lowest.

03:39.190 --> 03:43.550
And you can see 2013 is the best year in our business.

03:43.550 --> 03:47.190
And then it is declined massively in 2014.

03:47.230 --> 03:51.870
And of course we can go and add more measures to our data, not only the total sales.

03:51.870 --> 03:58.870
For example, let's go and calculate the total number of customers so we can say count distinct customer

03:58.910 --> 04:01.790
key as total customers.

04:02.030 --> 04:03.430
So let's go and execute it.

04:03.430 --> 04:07.110
And with that we can check our regaining like customers over the time.

04:07.150 --> 04:12.210
If there are any trends that we can see and we can go and keep extending stuff, like we can go and

04:12.210 --> 04:14.410
add the total number of quantities.

04:14.410 --> 04:18.770
So summarize quantity as total quantity.

04:19.130 --> 04:20.490
So let's go and execute.

04:20.530 --> 04:22.250
And with that we have a really nice picture.

04:22.250 --> 04:27.490
In order to understand is the revenue increasing or decreasing over the time.

04:27.490 --> 04:28.930
What is the best year.

04:28.930 --> 04:29.810
The worst year?

04:29.850 --> 04:31.930
Are we gaining customers over time?

04:31.970 --> 04:35.410
Is there any like trends that we can spot now by looking to the results?

04:35.410 --> 04:39.650
You can see this gives us high level, long term view of your data.

04:39.650 --> 04:42.530
And of course it helps for strategic decisions.

04:42.570 --> 04:47.530
And now what we can do, we can go and drill down to the months so we can go and aggregate the data

04:47.530 --> 04:53.970
by the month regardless the years, in order to give us an idea how each month is performing on average.

04:53.970 --> 04:58.530
So all what you have to do is to switch the function from year to a month like this.

04:58.570 --> 05:02.370
And of course for the groupby and the order by let's go and execute.

05:02.410 --> 05:05.210
And of course in the output we will get all the months.

05:05.210 --> 05:06.010
And guess what?

05:06.010 --> 05:12.290
Which months is the best for sales is of course December because you have all those Christmas and stuff.

05:12.290 --> 05:15.230
And the worst months as you can see, is February.

05:15.230 --> 05:21.430
So with that, we are understanding the seasonality of our business and the trends patterns of our business.

05:21.430 --> 05:27.270
And as you are not including the year in our analysis, you are aggregating all the data from all years.

05:27.310 --> 05:33.470
Now, what we can do, we can make it more specific for each year where you go and add the year informations

05:33.470 --> 05:36.750
to our query, so we can have both year and month.

05:36.790 --> 05:38.670
Let me just change this to a month.

05:39.030 --> 05:43.390
And of course we have to go and add it to the group by and the order by.

05:43.430 --> 05:45.030
So let's go and execute.

05:45.030 --> 05:48.790
And with that we are aggregating the data of a month of a specific year.

05:48.830 --> 05:51.790
So now we have all the months of all years.

05:51.830 --> 05:56.750
And now if you want to focus on only one year what you can do, you can go and filter the data by the

05:56.750 --> 05:57.510
order year.

05:57.510 --> 06:00.190
And with that you can see how the data is evolving over time.

06:00.230 --> 06:03.870
Now of course in SQL we can go and format the date differently.

06:03.870 --> 06:09.550
So instead of using the year and the month in separate columns, what we can do, we can use the date

06:09.590 --> 06:10.710
trunk function.

06:10.710 --> 06:13.030
So instead of here we're going to say date trunk.

06:13.070 --> 06:18.300
And if you want the granularity of your date at the month level you're going to say months and then

06:18.540 --> 06:19.380
the dates.

06:19.380 --> 06:23.460
And with that you will get both the year and the date.

06:23.460 --> 06:25.380
And let's call it order date.

06:25.380 --> 06:26.100
Like this.

06:26.260 --> 06:27.900
So let's go and execute.

06:27.940 --> 06:31.580
Now at the output we will get exactly the same result as before.

06:31.620 --> 06:35.420
But instead of having like two columns for the year and the month, we have everything in one.

06:35.420 --> 06:40.220
And because we said the month, that means it's going to go and remove all the days.

06:40.220 --> 06:42.780
So as you can see, it always starts with the one.

06:42.780 --> 06:44.620
So the first day of the month.

06:44.620 --> 06:48.500
And with that you will get one row for each month for each year.

06:48.540 --> 06:54.460
And if you want to change that quickly to year, just you go and change the date parts to here and you

06:54.500 --> 06:56.260
will get the granularity of the year.

06:56.300 --> 07:01.420
Now, if you don't like this format and you would like to have your specific format, what you can do,

07:01.420 --> 07:04.020
you can go and use the format function.

07:04.020 --> 07:07.580
So format the first argument is going to be the date.

07:07.620 --> 07:10.060
And then you go and do your format that you want.

07:10.100 --> 07:12.620
So for example it starts with the years.

07:12.660 --> 07:16.260
And let's say I would like to have the abbreviation of the month name.

07:16.260 --> 07:17.220
So something like this.

07:17.260 --> 07:20.360
And of course group by and order by.

07:20.400 --> 07:22.120
So let's go and execute it.

07:22.120 --> 07:26.360
And with that we got our format the year minus the abbreviation of the month.

07:26.360 --> 07:31.000
But here you have to be careful which function you are using because the formats you will get in the

07:31.000 --> 07:32.120
output string.

07:32.120 --> 07:34.760
And as you can see you cannot sort it correctly.

07:34.760 --> 07:38.000
So the data here is sorted by the year but not by the month.

07:38.000 --> 07:42.640
But if you are using date trunk you can see the data is correctly sorted.

07:42.640 --> 07:46.320
So if we switch it to a month it will be as well.

07:46.360 --> 07:52.240
Okay, so everything is sorted correctly because the output here is a date and the SQL is going to sort

07:52.280 --> 07:53.240
the date correctly.

07:53.240 --> 07:54.280
It is not string.

07:54.280 --> 07:58.840
And if you are using the year and the month, the output here is going to be an integer.

07:58.880 --> 08:01.080
And sorting an integer is not a problem.

08:01.080 --> 08:03.720
So of course you can go and pick the one that you like.

08:03.720 --> 08:04.680
So that's it.

08:04.680 --> 08:06.280
Let's go and execute it.

08:06.280 --> 08:10.760
And now you can go and keep analyzing by finding another date in our data set.

08:10.800 --> 08:12.080
And another measure.

08:12.080 --> 08:14.440
So as you can see it is very simple okay.

08:14.480 --> 08:18.120
So that's all about how to analyze the trends and the change over time.

08:18.120 --> 08:23.480
Now in the next step we are going to do some kind of advanced aggregations by doing cumulative analysis.
